"Internal Error ()\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ilearn.med.monash.edu.au/physiology/experiments/vision/blind-spot\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://ilearn.med.monash.edu.au/physiology/experiments/vision/blind-spot: UnexpectedStatusCode\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMissing Fundamental Demonstration (https://personalpages.manchester.ac.uk/staff/richard.baker/WebDemonstrations/Pitch/MissingFundamental.html)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://personalpages.manchester.ac.uk/staff/richard.baker/WebDemonstrations/Pitch/MissingFundamental.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 38\nL0: # Pitch of the Missing Fundamental\nL1: \nL2: Adjust the fundamental frequency and select the lowest and highest harmonics in the complex. Compare the fixed-time waveform, the frequency spectrum and the pitch that you hear.\nL3: \nL4: Fundamental frequency: 200 Hz [Input]\nL5: \nL6: 100 Hz 500 Hz\nL7: \nL8: Lowest harmonic present: 1 [Input]\nL9: \nL10: H1 H20\nL11: \nL12: Highest harmonic present: 20 [Input]\nL13: \nL14: H1 H20\nL15: \nL16: [Button: Play audio] [Button: Stop] [Button: Reset] Volume [Input] 60% Stopped\nL17: \nL18: ## Waveform\nL19: ## Spectrum\nL20: \nL21: Harmonics present\nL22: \nL23: H1-H20\nL24: \nL25: Lowest frequency present\nL26: \nL27: 200 Hz\nL28: \nL29: Highest frequency present\nL30: \nL31: 4000 Hz\nL32: \nL33: Predicted residue pitch\nL34: \nL35: 200 Hz\nL36: \nL37: Teaching point: raise the lowest harmonic to remove the fundamental and other low harmonics, or lower the highest harmonic to remove upper harmonics. The spectral region and waveform shape change, but harmonics that remain are separated by the fundamental frequency. A fixed 20 ms time axis makes the shorter period at higher fundamental frequencies clearly visible.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Birthday Problem - explanation, MSTE, University of Illinois (https://mste.illinois.edu/activity/birthday/explanation.php)\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://mste.illinois.edu/activity/birthday/explanation.php\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 42\nL0:                                  cite0†College of Education†education.illinois.edu cite1†Office for Mathematics, Science, and Technology Education L1: \nL2:   * cite1†Home L3:   * About\nL4:     * cite2†About MSTE L5:     * cite3†MSTE Personnel L6:   * cite4†Resources L7:   * Projects\nL8:     * cite5†Featured Projects L9:     * cite6†Past Projects L10:   * Contact\nL11:     * cite7†Contact MSTE L12:     * cite8†Directions to MSTE L13:     * cite9†Contact Form L14: \nL15: cite1†Home cite10†Activity cite11†Birthday Explanation\nL16: # The Birthday Problem - explanation\nL17: \nL18: Let's look at the probabilities a step at a time.\nL19:   * For one person, there are 365 distinct birthdays.\nL20:   * For two people, there are 364 different ways that the second could have a birthday without matching the first.\nL21:   * If there is no match after two people, the third person has 363 different birthdays that do not match the other two. So, the probability of a match is 1 - (365)(364)(363)/(365)(365)(365).\nL22:   * This leads to the following formula for calculating the probability of a match with N birthdays is 1 - (365)(364)(363)...(365 - N + 1)/(365)^N.\nL23: Running this through a computer gives the chart below. Notice that a probability of over .5 is obtained after 23 dates!\nL24: cite14†Image: Chart of birthday probabilities with Number of Birthdays along X-axis and Probability of a Match along Y-axis. The graph curves up, rising quickly from 0,0 to 50% probability by 23 people, and slowly leveling off after about 40 people at above 90% probability. By 60 people, the probability is nearly 100%. L25: Notice that the probability is above .9 before the sample size reaches even 45.\nL26: \nL27: Return to the cite11†Introduction .\nL28: cite1†Office for Mathematics, Science, and Technology Education cite0†College of Education†education.illinois.edu L29: \nL30:   *   *   *\nL31: ## Explore:\nL32: \nL33:   * cite4†Resources L34:   * cite12†Projects L35: \nL36: ## Connect:\nL37: \nL38:   * cite13†About Us L39:   * cite7†Contact Us L40: \nL41: Copyright © 1994-2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.si.edu/object/pneumatic-tube-canister%3Anpm_0.269514.1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.si.edu/object/pneumatic-tube-canister%3Anpm_0.269514.1: (404) Not Found\n"
